/home/muk/projects/siminfeng/decisions/D035-pregate-prefetch.md:36:- High recall alone did not win. J-OM10 pg8 loses to fill-only frd16; pg16 regresses even against base. The first hook queued next-layer IO before current-layer demand; stale queued work survived arrival, and async reads could occupy the whole worker pool. This created competing and duplicate IO (`06-pregate.patch`; `c2/worklog.jsonl`, J-OM10-ab result).
/home/muk/projects/siminfeng/decisions/D035-pregate-prefetch.md:38:- Keep pg8b as the best supported configuration: D034 fill settings plus `LLAMA_EXPERT_PREGATE=1 LLAMA_EXPERT_PREGATE_K=8 LLAMA_EXPERT_PREGATE_ORDER=after LLAMA_EXPERT_PREGATE_MAX_INFLIGHT=4` (`experiments/jetson/OM/J-OM10b/spec.json`, `J-OM10c/spec.json`). J-OM10b gives 1.42 t/s in both repeats, versus fill-only 1.29/1.28. J-OM10c reproduces an improved result, but raising occupancy gives no clear incremental win within the reported noise. Each gate compares against base, not against the other candidates.
/home/muk/projects/siminfeng/decisions/D035-pregate-prefetch.md:40:- Prefetch spends speculative IO and cancels substantial backlog (table); it is not a byte-minimization win over fill-only. J-OM10b NVMe GiB/token rises from frd16 0.8098085494268508 to pg8b 0.852730221218533 (`experiments/jetson/OM/J-OM10b/REPORT.md`).
